WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.637
Let's turn motion into electricity, without pretending the magnetic field

00:00:06.637 --> 00:00:06.900
gives

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:07.980
us free energy.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.260
Picture a rod sliding right on two rails, with a field into the page.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.940
The loop gets bigger, so inward magnetic flux increases.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:17.900
What should the induced current do?

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:18.800
Take a moment.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.820
It produces an outward field to oppose that increase, so current is

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.800
counterclockwise, upward through the rod.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.900
The resulting force on the rod points left.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.700
You must pull right to keep the speed constant.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:36.200
Here is our example: field zero point five tesla, rod length zero point four

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.600
metres, speed three metres per second, and total resistance two ohms.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:44.820
Emf is B times length times speed: zero point six volts.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.440
Current is zero point three amperes.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.220
Braking force is B times current times length, zero point zero six newtons.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:56.780
Multiply by speed: mechanical input is zero point one eight watts.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.400
Current squared times resistance gives exactly the same heating power.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.900
Good, our energy account balances.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.660
We neglected friction and circuit inductance.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.800
Now rotate a rod about one end.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.860
Should we use the tip speed everywhere?

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:10.840
No.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.180
At distance s from the pivot, speed is omega times s.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.120
Integrating B omega s along the rod gives B omega length squared divided by

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:19.380
two.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.560
With zero point four tesla, twenty radians per second and half a metre, emf is

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.020
one volt.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.320
Counterclockwise rotation with field out of the page makes the tip positive.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.100
An isolated rod can have this voltage without continuous current.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.360
Add a stationary return path and contacts with five ohms total resistance, and

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:39.920
current is zero point two amperes.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.120
Braking torque is B times current times length squared divided by two: zero

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.360
point zero one newton metres.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.000
Torque times angular speed is zero point two watts, matching resistor heating.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.060
Different geometry, same conservation check.

00:01:54.312 --> 00:01:56.712
Next, let's give the circuit a little memory.

00:01:57.332 --> 00:01:58.972
An inductor stores magnetic energy.

00:01:59.772 --> 00:02:03.752
For constant inductance, its voltage is L times the rate of change of current,

00:02:04.292 --> 00:02:05.612
using the passive sign convention.

00:02:06.272 --> 00:02:10.052
A finite applied voltage changes the slope of current, not the current

00:02:10.052 --> 00:02:10.812
instantaneously.

00:02:11.872 --> 00:02:15.812
Imagine connecting a ten volt source to five ohms and a zero point five henry

00:02:15.812 --> 00:02:18.492
inductor in series, initially with zero current.

00:02:19.192 --> 00:02:21.372
Right at the start, current is still zero.

00:02:21.932 --> 00:02:25.852
The resistor voltage is zero, and the inductor initially takes the source

00:02:25.852 --> 00:02:26.212
voltage.

00:02:26.932 --> 00:02:28.532
Eventually the current stops changing.

00:02:29.152 --> 00:02:33.498
The ideal inductor then has zero voltage, and current approaches ten divided

00:02:33.498 --> 00:02:33.612
by

00:02:33.612 --> 00:02:35.032
five, two amperes.

00:02:35.632 --> 00:02:36.032
How quickly?

00:02:36.552 --> 00:02:39.992
The time constant is L divided by R, zero point one seconds.

00:02:40.772 --> 00:02:43.895
Growth current is the final current times one minus the exponential of

00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:44.252
negative

00:02:44.252 --> 00:02:45.792
time divided by the time constant.

00:02:46.492 --> 00:02:51.052
At one time constant, that factor is about zero point six three two.

00:02:51.052 --> 00:02:54.092
So current is about one point two six amperes, not two.

00:02:54.612 --> 00:02:58.052
Think of the time constant as a characteristic pace, not a finish line.

00:02:58.672 --> 00:03:02.732
The final stored magnetic energy is one half L times current squared: one

00:03:02.732 --> 00:03:03.272
joule.

00:03:03.672 --> 00:03:07.652
Now remove the source but leave a closed resistor and inductor discharge loop.

00:03:08.492 --> 00:03:12.092
Current decays exponentially, and the stored energy becomes heat in that

00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:12.352
resistor.

00:03:12.972 --> 00:03:15.952
But opening the only current path is a different situation.

00:03:16.652 --> 00:03:20.132
The inductor can develop a large voltage, sometimes causing a spark.

00:03:20.772 --> 00:03:24.272
We do not casually apply the closed loop decay formula to an open circuit.

00:03:25.112 --> 00:03:29.172
For JEE, sketch the circuit before and after switching, state the initial

00:03:29.172 --> 00:03:32.392
current, and identify the actual path available for discharge.

00:03:32.932 --> 00:03:36.152
That picture often matters more than memorizing another formula.

00:03:36.432 --> 00:03:38.172
Finally, an AC puzzle.

00:03:38.772 --> 00:03:42.452
Can a twenty volt source produce two hundred volts across an inductor in a

00:03:42.452 --> 00:03:42.812
passive circuit?

00:03:43.292 --> 00:03:47.612
At series resonance, yes, under the ideal conditions we are about to specify.

00:03:48.292 --> 00:03:53.312
Take inductance zero point two henry, capacitance twenty microfarads, total

00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:57.332
series resistance ten ohms, and source voltage twenty volts RMS.

00:03:58.352 --> 00:04:02.375
Resonant angular frequency is one divided by the square root of L times C:

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:02.592
five

00:04:02.592 --> 00:04:06.212
hundred radians per second, about seventy nine point five eight hertz.

00:04:07.212 --> 00:04:10.612
Inductive and capacitive reactances each have magnitude one hundred ohms.

00:04:11.192 --> 00:04:15.272
They have opposite phase, so they cancel in the total impedance, leaving ten

00:04:15.272 --> 00:04:15.512
ohms.

00:04:16.232 --> 00:04:17.832
Current is two amperes RMS.

00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:22.152
Each reactive element has two hundred volts RMS, but their voltage phasors

00:04:22.152 --> 00:04:22.612
oppose.

00:04:22.832 --> 00:04:26.032
We must not add those magnitudes as if they point in the same direction.

00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:29.732
The source supplies forty watts, dissipated in resistance.

00:04:30.672 --> 00:04:34.652
Ideal inductors and capacitors exchange energy but consume no average power.

00:04:35.352 --> 00:04:37.492
Now ask how selective this resonance is.

00:04:38.032 --> 00:04:42.092
With source voltage fixed, half resonant power means current is the resonant

00:04:42.092 --> 00:04:43.872
value divided by the square root of two.

00:04:44.412 --> 00:04:48.192
This happens when the magnitude of inductive reactance minus capacitive

00:04:48.192 --> 00:04:49.692
reactance equals resistance.

00:04:50.532 --> 00:04:53.961
Solving the lower and upper cases gives approximately four hundred seventy

00:04:53.961 --> 00:04:54.146
five

00:04:54.146 --> 00:04:58.792
point six two and five hundred twenty five point six two radians per second.

00:04:59.012 --> 00:05:03.872
Subtract: bandwidth is fifty radians per second, exactly R divided by L for

00:05:03.872 --> 00:05:05.032
this ideal series model.

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:10.012
Quality factor is resonant angular frequency times L divided by R, which is

00:05:10.012 --> 00:05:10.272
ten.

00:05:10.712 --> 00:05:11.572
Notice one subtlety.

00:05:12.052 --> 00:05:15.632
The geometric mean of the half power frequencies is the resonance frequency;

00:05:15.872 --> 00:05:18.512
their arithmetic midpoint is not exactly resonance.

00:05:19.272 --> 00:05:21.952
Symmetric endpoints are an approximation, not an identity.

00:05:22.672 --> 00:05:25.632
Real losses and component ratings limit voltage magnification.

00:05:26.252 --> 00:05:30.145
Our routine is to name the model, keep RMS separate from peak values, and

00:05:30.145 --> 00:05:30.412
check

00:05:30.412 --> 00:05:30.712
power.

00:05:31.172 --> 00:05:34.772
That is how these equations become useful tools rather than a guessing game.
